DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20220127
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Seacoast Modular Homes\, Inc. v. Chandler O. 
 Dalzell\, Jr.\, and Jeanne Dalzell
DESCRIPTION:1 \n \nSTATE OF MAINE             BUSINESS & CONSUMER COURT \n
 CUMBERLAND\, ss.             DOCKET NO. BCD-RE-2020-00008 \n \nSEACOAST MO
 DULAR HOMES\, \nINC.\,  \n \n                      Plaintiff\, \n \n      
     v. \n \nCHANDLER O. DALZELL\, JR.\, and \nJEANNE DALZELL\, \n \n      
                 Defendants. \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n \n 
 \n \n \nORDER DENYING PLAINTIFF'S \nMOTION FOR PARTIAL SUMMARY \nJUDGMENT 
 \n \n)  \n \nINTRODUCTION \nThis is a case between homeowners and the comp
 any with which they contracted to \nconstruct their new home. Plaintiff Se
 acoast Modular Homes\, Inc. (“SMH”) brings several claims \nagainst De
 fendants Chandler O. Dalzell\, Jr.\, and Jeanne Dalzell (together\, the 
 “Dalzells”)\, seeking \nto collect payment on the home SMH constructed
  for the Dalzells in York County\, Maine. The \nmatter presently before th
 e Court is SMH’s Motion for Partial Summary Judgment (the “Motion”) 
 \non Count V of SMH’s complaint for violation of Maine’s Prompt Paymen
 t Act\, 10 M.R.S. § 1111-\n1120 (the “Prompt Payment Act” or the “A
 ct”). Because there are genuine disputes of material \nfact\, as discuss
 ed below the Court DENIES summary judgment to Plaintiff.1 \nSTANDARD OF RE
 VIEW \nSummary judgment is appropriate where the parties’ statements of 
 material fact and the \nportions of the record referenced therein “discl
 ose no genuine issues of material fact and reveal \nthat one party is enti
 tled to judgment as a matter of law.” Currie v. Indus. Sec.\, Inc.\, 200
 7 ME 12\, \n¶ 11\, 915 A.2d 400. “A material fact is one that can affec
 t the outcome of the case\, and there is a \n \n1 The Court decides the Mo
 tion without hearing as provided by M.R. Civ. P. 7( b)(7) \n2 \n \ngenuine
  issue when there is sufficient evidence for a fact finder to choose  betw
 een competing \nversions of the fact.” Lougee Conservancy v. CitiMortgag
 e\, Inc.\, 2012 ME 103\, ¶ 11\, 48 A.3d 774 \n(quoting Stewart-Dore v. We
 bber Hos p. Ass'n\, 2011 ME 26\, ¶ 8\, 13 A.3d 773). The Court must \nvie
 w a party’s statements of material fact in the light most favorabl\nhttp
 s://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/bcd/re-orders/RE-20-08/BCD-RE-20-08-Order-
 Denying-Motion-for-Partial-Summary-Judgment.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/bcd/re-orders/RE-20-08/BCD-RE-20-0
 8-Order-Denying-Motion-for-Partial-Summary-Judgment.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b486a54d5cd98586@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260802T111844Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20220118
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] 800 Northern Corp. v. Congress Federal Realty
 \, LLC\, as Trustee of Congress Federal Trust
DESCRIPTION:1 \n \nSTATE OF MAINE             BUSINESS & CONSUMER COURT \n
 CUMBERLAND\, ss.             DOCKET NO. BCD-CIV-2021-00060 \n \n800 NORTHE
 RN CORP.\,  \n \n                      Plaintiff\, \n \n          v. \n \n
 CONGRESS FEDERAL REALTY\, LLC\, as \nTrustee of CONGRESS FEDERAL TRUST\, \
 n \n                      Defendant. \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \
 n) \n \n \n \n \nORDER DENYING PLAINTIFF'S \nAMENDED MOTION FOR \nATTACHME
 NT \n \n  \nINTRODUCTION \nThe matter presently before the Court is a n Am
 ended Motion for Attachment brought by \nPlaintiff 800 Northern Corp. (“
 800 Northern”) under M.R. Civ. P. 4A. In its Motion\, 800 Northern \ncon
 tends it is more likely than not to succeed on the merits of  its claims f
 or breach of co venant\, \npromissory estoppel\, and quantum meruit. 800 N
 ort hern seeks an attachment against Defendant \nCongress Federal Realty\,
  LLC (“Congress Federal”) in the amount of $249\,262\, which consists 
 of \n$146\,789 for what 800 Northern characterizes as Maintenance Reimburs
 ement\, and $102\,473 for \nwhat it characterizes as Sidewalk Reimbursemen
 t. Congress Federal attacks both the merits of the \nMotion and the amount
  sought to be attached. Oral argument was held on January 18\, 2022. For \
 nthe reasons discussed below\, the Court DENIES Plaintiff’s Motion. \nFA
 CTUAL ALLEGATIONS \n 800 Northern owns the property and office building lo
 cated at 2 Monument Square in \ndowntown Portland\, Maine  (“Two Monumen
 t Sq.”)  (Pl.’s Am. Compl. ¶ 3.) Congress Federal \nowns the adjacent
  property and building located at 1 Monument Square (“One Monument Sq.
 ”). \n(Am. Compl. ¶ 4.) The two buildings share a common lobby (the “
 Lobby”) and service driveway \n(the “Driveway”) located on 800 North
 ern’s property and over which Congress Federal has \n2 \n \neasement rig
 hts to use. ( Am. Compl. ¶ ¶ 1\, 18\, 20.) Longstanding deeded covenants
  require \nCongress Federal to reimburse 800 Northern for half of costs in
 curred to maintain these common \nareas. (Am. Compl. ¶ 1\; Am. Compl. Exs
 . A-C.)  \n The original “Modification\, Easement and \nhttps://www.cour
 ts.maine.gov/courts/bcd/cv-orders/CIV-21-60/BCD-CIV-21-60-Order-on-Motion-
 for-Attachment.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/bcd/cv-orders/CIV-21-60/BCD-CIV-21
 -60-Order-on-Motion-for-Attachment.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
